\h Exodus
\toc1 Exodus
\toc2 Exodus
\toc3 Exo.
\mt1 Exodus
\is1 Introduction
\ip The word \bk Exodus\bk* comes from the Greek word meaning ‘departure’ or ‘leaving’. Hebrew readers call this document ‘Shemōt’ which is the second word in the Hebrew and means ‘Names’ because it begins with a list of names. Some European and other translations call it ‘The second book of Mosheh/Moses’.
\ip It should be noted that ‘Mosheh’ is the best English transliteration of the name of the hero of this account. Sadly, he's commonly known in the English world as ‘Moses’, even though we have all the sounds in our language to be able to pronounce his name more accurately. Unfortunately, because the language of ‘the New Testament’ didn't have the ‘sh’ sound and had no letter for ‘h’, his Hebrew version of his Egyptian name is corrupted as it goes through Greek ‘Mōsaʸs’ and then Latin ‘Moyses’ and then first entered English in the 1300's as ‘Moises’. Now that we live in a modern era where we try to show more respect to the names of peoples from other cultures, the \em OET\em* encourages the same for Biblical characters (especially the major ones), hence ‘Mosheh’. (Maybe a little bit of relearning could demonstrate a lot of respect to the Jewish people whose ancestors are recorded in these accounts.)
\ip After God had used a famine to cause his people to go down into Egypt (Heb. Mitsrayim), this document records how they eventually become slaves, but then God works many miracles to enable them to leave again, and not only that, to be paid at the end for their work.
\ip This story can be divided into three main parts: 1. their slavery in Egypt, and how God called Mosheh (Moses), the child who had been adopted by an Egyptian princess, to lead them out of slavery; 2. how God assisted them when they were chased by the Egyptian army, then when they reached Mt. Sinai, how they were miraculously given food and drink there at the mountain; 3. how Yahweh revealed himself to Mosheh there at Mt. Sinai, and gave him instructions to guide their lives and their nation, and God's agreement with the people; 4. the ending is the construction of the sacred tent, complete with instructions about how they should worship.
\ip An overview would be how God rescued his people from slavery and established them as a nation that was hopeful of a good future.
\ip \em Mosheh\em* is the most important person in this account. He was chosen by God to lead his people out of slavery and right out of Egypt. The \em ten commands\em* are another well-known component of this document and can be found in chapter twenty.
\iot Main components of this account
\io1 Yisrael's descendants are driven out of Egypt \ior 1:1–15:21\ior*
\io2 a. The people are enslaved there in Egypt \ior 1:1-22\ior*
\io2 b. The birth and early life of Mosheh \ior 2:1–4:31\ior*
\io2 c. Far'oh (Pharaoh) king of Egypt stands strongly against Mosheh and Aharon taking the people out of Egypt \ior 5:1–11:10\ior*
\io2 d. God's messenger of death that ‘passed-over’, and the exit of Yisra'el's descendants from Egypt \ior 12:1–15:21\ior*
\io1 Leaving the Red Sea and going to Mt. Sinai \ior 15:22–18:27\ior*
\io1 God's instructions and the agreement \ior 19:1–24:18\ior*
\io1 The sacred tent and warnings concerning worshipping \ior 25:1–40:38\ior*
\rem This is still an early look into the unpolished text of the Open English Translation of the Bible. Please double-check the text in advance before using in public.
\ie
\c 1
\s1 Yisra'el's descendants are driven into slavery
\rem /s1 The Israelites Are Treated Cruelly in Egypt; The Israelites Oppressed; The Israelites in Egypt
\p
\v 1 These¦25401 are¦25402 the¦25402 names¦25402 of¦25403 Yisra'el's¦25404 descendants¦25403 who¦25405 accompanied Yacob¦25408 (Jacob) when he moved his¦25410 household¦25410 to Egypt¦25406:\x + \xo 1:1-4: \xt Gen 46:8-27.\x*
\v 2 Reuben¦25413, Simeon¦25414, Levi¦25415, Yehudah¦25416,
\v 3 Yissakar¦25418 (Issachar¦25418), Zebulun¦25419, Benyamin¦25420,
\v 4 Dan¦25423, Naftali¦25424, Gad¦25425, and¦25424 Asher¦25426.
\v 5 \add At that time,\add* Yacob¦25435 had a total of¦25432 seventy¦25436 descendants (including¦25438 Yosef¦25438 \add and his sons\add* who¦25432 were¦25439 \add already\add* in¦25440 Egypt¦25440.)
\p
\v 6 \add As the years progressed,\add* Yosef¦25443 and¦25442 his¦25446 brothers¦25446 died¦25442—all¦25444 of¦25446 that¦25449 generation¦25448,
\v 7 \add but they'd had many children who went on to have their own children and grandchildren,\add* and so they became¦25456 a large and powerful¦25456 group \add there in Egypt\add*.\x + \xo 1:7: \xt Acts 7:17.\x*
\rem /s1 The Israelites Are Oppressed
\p
\v 8 \add Eventually\add* a new¦25467 king¦25465 came to power in Egypt¦25470 \add !(Mitsrayim¦25470)\add* who¦25471 had never known¦25474 Yosef¦25477\x + \xo 1:8: \xt Acts 7:18.\x*
\v 9 and¦25479 he¦25479 told¦25479 his¦25482 people¦25482, “Listen¦25483, those descendants of¦25482 Yisrael¦25486 \add !(Israel¦25486)\add* are¦25487 becoming more¦25489 numerous¦25487 and¦25488 powerful¦25488 than¦25489 us¦25489.
\v 10 \add So\add*, let's¦25492 deal¦25492 wisely¦25492 with¦25511 them, in¦25511 case they \add continue to\add* multiply¦25496. Then if¦25498 war¦25501 was¦25497 to break out, they could easily take the¦25515 other side and fight¦25509 against¦25511 us¦25508, and then leave¦25512 our country¦25515.”\x + \xo 1:10: \xt Acts 7:19.\x*
\v 11 Then¦25517 they¦25517 appointed¦25517 overseers¦25519 to¦25522 work¦25523 them in¦25523 slave¦25520 gangs¦25520, and using them as¦25521 forced¦25523 labour¦25523, they built¦25524 the store¦25526 cities¦25525 at¦25523 Pithon¦25530 and¦25531 Rameses¦25533 for¦25527 Far'oh¦25527 (Pharaoh).
\v 12 \add However,\add* the more they oppressed¦25536 them, the more they multiplied¦25539 and¦25540 spread¦25541 out, and¦25542 \add the more\add* the Egyptians came¦25542 to dread¦25542 the¦25544 Israelis¦25545.
\v 13 \add So they\add* made the¦25551 Israelis¦25552 work¦25547 \add long, hard hours\add*,
\v 14 and¦25555 they¦25555 made¦25555 their¦25558 lives¦25558 bitter¦25555 with¦25559 the hard¦25560 slavery. \add They were forced to make\add* mortar¦25561 and¦25562 bricks¦25562, as well as all kinds of¦25558 slave labour¦25573 in¦25559 the¦25566 fields¦25566—all¦25568 the work¦25570 they¦25573 had to do was \add strenuous and tiring\add*.
\p
\v 15 Then¦25577 the¦25578 king¦25578 of¦25578 Egypt¦25579 spoke¦25577 to¦25580 Shifrah¦25585 and¦25586 Puah¦25588, the two Hebrew¦25581 midwives¦25580,
\v 16 saying¦25590, “When¦25590 you¦25591 assist¦25591 the¦25594 Hebrew¦25594 women¦25594 as midwives¦25591, \add as you sit there on your\add* stool, if you see¦25595 that it's a male, do \add something\add* so he'll die, but¦25590 if it's¦25590 a female, then¦25590 let her live¦25609.”
\v 17 But¦25611 the¦25612 midwives¦25612 \add were godly women\add* and¦25616 didn't¦25616 do¦25617 according to¦25620 what the¦25621 Egyptian¦25622 king¦25621 \add had instructed\add* them¦25620, and they¦25617 let¦25623 the boys live¦25623.
\v 18 \add So\add* the¦25629 king¦25629 called¦25628 the midwives¦25632 \add back\add*, and¦25633 he¦25633 \add demanded\add*, “Why¦25631 have¦25636 you done¦25636 that¦25638 and¦25639 let¦25639 \add their\add* sons live¦25639?”
\p
\v 19 “Because¦25649 the Hebrew¦25653 women aren't¦25650 like¦25651 Egyptian¦25652 women¦25653,” the midwives¦25661 \add ≈answered¦25644\add*. “They're¦25648 \add more active\add* and¦25644 give¦25662 birth¦25662 before¦25658 the midwife¦25661 \add even\add* gets to¦25660 them¦25660.”
\v 20 \add So\add* God¦25665 rewarded the midwives¦25666, and¦25667 \add meanwhile,\add* the¦25668 people¦25668 \add continued to increase in number\add* and¦25669 become¦25669 more powerful¦25669,
\v 21 and¦25672 because¦25673 the¦25676 midwives¦25676 had \add demonstrated their desire to obey\add* God¦25677, he¦25672 gave them¦25681 families¦25682 \add of their own\add*.
\v 22 Then¦25684 Far'oh¦25685 commanded¦25684 all¦25686 of¦25688 his¦25688 people¦25688, “You¦25694 \add all need to\add* toss¦25695 every¦25690 newborn boy¦25692 into¦25694 the¦25694 river¦25694, but¦25684 you can let¦25699 the girls live¦25699.”\x + \xo 1:22: \xt Acts 7:19.\x*
\c 2
\s1 The birth of Mosheh (Moses)
\rem /s1 Moses; The Birth of Moses; Birth and Youth of Moses
\p
\v 1 \add During this period,\add* a man¦25703 from the¦25704 tribe of¦25705 Levi¦25705 married a woman who was also¦25702 a Levite¦25711,
\v 2 and¦25713 she¦25713 conceived¦25713 and¦25715 gave¦25715 birth¦25715 to a son¦25716. When¦25718 she saw¦25717 that¦25719 he¦25722 was¦25721 a \add healthy\add* boy¦25716, she hid¦25723 him¦25723 away for¦25719 three¦25724 months¦25725,\x + \xo 2:2: \xt Acts 7:20; Heb 11:23.\x*
\v 3 \add but\add* then¦25727 she¦25729 wasn't able¦25729 to¦25731 hide¦25731 him¦25731 any longer. \add So\add* she¦25732 got¦25732 a¦25735 basket¦25735 made from \add woven\add* reeds and¦25737 plastered¦25737 it¦25737 with¦25738 bitumen¦25738 and¦25739 pitch¦25739. Then¦25727 she put¦25740 the¦25744 baby¦25744 in it, and¦25745 floated \add the basket\add* in the¦25749 reeds along the¦25750 riverbank¦25750,
\v 4 \add leaving the baby's\add* sister¦25753 to watch from¦25754 a distance¦25754 to¦25755 see¦25755 what¦25756 would¦25758 happen to¦25759 him¦25759.
\p
\v 5 \add After a while,\add* Far'oh's¦25764 daughter¦25762 came¦25761 down¦25761 to¦25765 the river¦25774 to wash \add herself\add*, and \add she and\add* her young \add attendants\add* were¦25770 walking¦25770 along the \add riverbank\add*. She¦25775 saw¦25775 the container among the reeds¦25780 and¦25781 sent¦25781 \add one of\add* her slave¦25784 women to get it¦25785.
\v 6 She¦25787 opened¦25787 it¦25797 and saw¦25788 the¦25791 baby inside, and¦25792 wow, he \add started\add* crying¦25795. She¦25796 felt sorry¦25796 for him¦25788 and said¦25798, “This¦25801 baby \add must be\add* one¦25799 of¦25799 the Hebrew¦25800 children¦25799.”
\p
\v 7 Then¦25803 his¦25804 sister¦25804 \add approached and\add* asked¦25803 \add the princess\add*, “Would¦25809 you like me to go¦25810 and¦25811 find a¦25813 Hebrew¦25816 woman¦25813 who'll be able to breastfeed¦25814 the baby¦25821 for¦25818 you?”
\p
\v 8 “\add Yes,\add* go¦25829,” answered¦25823 Far'oh's¦25828 daughter¦25826, and¦25830 the¦25831 girl¦25831 went¦25830 and¦25832 got the¦25835 baby's mother¦25835.
\v 9 “Take¦25843 this¦25847 baby,” said¦25838 the \add princess\add*, “and¦25848 breastfeed him¦25848 for me¦25849, and¦25850 I'll¦25851 pay¦25851 you \add for doing it\add*.” \add So\add* the¦25856 woman¦25856 took¦25855 the¦25857 baby and¦25858 \add looked after\add* him¦25858.
\v 10 \add When\add* the boy¦25870 had grown¦25860 \add enough\add*, she¦25863 brought¦25863 him¦25863 \add back\add* to¦25864 Far'oh's¦25866 daughter¦25864 and¦25860 he¦25867 became her¦25869 son¦25870. She¦25871 named¦25871 him ‘Mosheh¦25873’\f + \fr 2:10 \ft More familiar to most English readers as ‘Moses’ from the Greek ‘Μωσῆς’ (Mōsaʸs) but Greek doesn't have an ‘h’ or a ‘sh’ so by going through Greek we ended up with something quite different from his real name. However, English does have those sounds and letters, so there's no reason why we can't get this name correct.\f* \add (which means ‘pulled out’)\add* because¦25875 she said¦25874 that she'd \add plucked\add* him out of¦25864 the¦25878 \add river\add*.\x + \xo 2:10: \xt Acts 7:21.\x*
\s1 Mosheh escapes to Midyan
\rem /s1 Moses Escapes to Midian; Moses Flees to Midian
\p
\v 11 \add Later on\add* when Mosheh¦25886 was¦25881 fully grown¦25885, he¦25887 went¦25887 out¦25887 to¦25888 visit \add the Hebrews\add* and¦25891 saw¦25891 their¦25892 \add forced labour\add*, and¦25893 he¦25893 saw¦25893 an Egyptian¦25895 man beating¦25896 a Hebrew¦25899 man—one¦25900 of¦25900 his¦25900 own people.\x + \xo 2:11-14: \xt Acts 7:23-28.\x*
\v 12 \add Mosheh\add* looked¦25902 around to check that¦25906 no one was¦25907 watching, then he¦25909 hit¦25909 the¦25912 Egyptian¦25912, \add killing him,\add* then he¦25913 hid¦25913 his body in¦25914 the sand¦25914.
\v 13 The¦25917 next day¦25917, he went¦25916 out¦25916 again and¦25919 wow, two¦25920 Hebrew¦25923 men¦25922 were¦25924 fighting¦25924 each other, and¦25925 he¦25925 said¦25925 to¦25926 the man¦25922 in¦25917 the¦25918 wrong¦25926, “Why are¦25928 you¦25928 hitting¦25928 your¦25929 \add fellow Hebrew\add*?”
\p
\v 14 “Who¦25932 made you the ruler and¦25931 judge¦25936 over¦25937 us¦25937?” \add the man replied\add*. “Are¦25939 you \add planning\add* to¦25938 kill¦25938 me¦25938 like¦25941 you¦25942 killed¦25942 \add that\add* Egyptian¦25945?” Then¦25931 Mosheh¦25947 was¦25946 afraid¦25946 because he realised¦25940 that what he'd done¦25933 had \add probably become widely\add* known¦25950.
\v 15 \add Indeed,\add* when Far'oh¦25954 heard¦25953 about¦25966 it¦25953, he¦25959 \add ordered\add* Mosheh to¦25960 be killed¦25960. \add So\add* Mosheh¦25963 had to flee¦25964 from¦25966 \add the king\add* and¦25968 he¦25968 \add took off east\add* to live¦25968 in¦25969 Midian¦25971 and¦25972 he¦25972 stayed near the¦25975 well¦25975.\x + \xo 2:15: \xt Acts 7:29; Heb 11:27.\x*
\p
\v 16 The¦25984 priest¦25977 there in Midian¦25978 had seven¦25979 daughters¦25980, and¦25981 they¦25981 would come¦25981 to the well to draw¦25982 water¦25987 out and fill¦25983 up the¦25986 troughs¦25986 there so their¦25989 father's¦25989 \add sheep and goats\add* could drink¦25986.
\v 17 Now¦25998 some male shepherds¦25992 came¦25991 along and \add started to\add* shoo their flock¦26000 away¦25993, but¦25991 Mosheh¦25995 got involved and¦25996 helped them¦25996 so that their¦26000 animals could drink.
\v 18 \add When\add* they¦26002 \add got home\add* to¦26003 their¦26006 father¦26006 Reuel¦26005, he¦26007 \add asked\add*, “How come¦26010 you¦26009 \add got home\add* so early today?”
\p
\v 19 “There was an Egyptian¦26015 man¦26014,” they¦26013 \add ≈answered¦26013\add*, “who stood up for us¦26016 against those \add other\add* shepherds¦26018. And¦26019 he¦26022 even drew¦26022 \add water\add* for us and¦26013 gave our flock¦26027 water¦26024 to¦26023 drink.”
\p
\v 20 “Where¦26033 is¦26033 he \add now\add*?” he \add asked them\add*. “What's¦26034 this—you mean you all just¦26035 left¦26036 him there? Go and¦26029 get him¦26041 \add so we can give him a meal\add*.”
\p
\v 21 \add Later it turned out that\add* Mosheh¦26046 was¦26045 prepared to¦26047 live¦26047 with¦26048 the¦26050 man¦26050, and¦26051 \add in due course\add* he¦26051 gave¦26051 his¦26055 daughter¦26055 Zipporah¦26054 to¦26056 Mosheh¦26056 \add in marriage\add*.
\v 22 \add When\add* she¦26058 gave¦26058 birth¦26058 to a son¦26059, he¦26060 named¦26063 him ‘Gershom¦26064’ \add (which means ‘foreigner’)\add* because¦26065 he¦26066 said¦26066, “I've¦26068 become¦26068 a foreigner¦26070 \add living\add* in¦26069 a foreign¦26070 land¦26069.”
\p
\v 23 \add Eventually\add* Egypt's¦26079 king¦26078 died¦26077, \add but\add* the¦26081 Israelis¦26083 groaned¦26080 from¦26084 the¦26086 slavery \add they were still under\add* and¦26087 they¦26087 cried¦26087 out¦26087, and their¦26089 cry¦26089 \add for freedom\add* from slavery went¦26088 up¦26088 to¦26090 God¦26092.
\v 24 \add He\add* heard¦26097 their¦26101 groaning¦26101 and¦26102 remembered¦26102 his¦26106 agreement¦26106 with¦26107 Abraham¦26109, with¦26110 Yitshak¦26112, and¦26113 with¦26113 Yacob¦26115,\x + \xo 2:24: \xt Gen 15:13-14.\x*
\v 25 and¦26117 he¦26117 \add looked down on\add* the¦26121 Israelis¦26122 and¦26123 he¦26123 \add was concerned about them\add*.
\c 3
\s1 God calls Mosheh from a burning bush
\rem /s1 God Calls Moses; Moses at the Burning Bush; Moses and the Burning Bush
\p
\v 1 \add One time\add* Mosheh¦26127 (Moses) was¦26128 shepherding the¦26132 flock¦26132 of his¦26134 father-in-law¦26134 Yetro (or Jethro, the¦26135 priest¦26135 at Midian¦26136), and¦26137 he¦26137 led the¦26140 flock¦26140 beyond the¦26142 wilderness¦26142 and¦26143 came¦26143 to¦26144 a hill¦26146. (\add This was later known as\add* the mountain¦26146 of¦26146 God¦26147 at Horev¦26148).
\v 2 \add While he was there.\add* Yahweh's¦26152 messenger¦26151 appeared¦26150 to him¦26153 in¦26154 a flame¦26154 coming from¦26157 the¦26157 middle¦26157 of¦26157 a bush, and¦26150 as \add Mosheh\add* looked¦26150, to his surprise \add he saw that\add* the bush was¦26162 burning¦26162 in the fire¦26163 yet not¦26165 \add actually\add* being burnt¦26162 up.\x + \xo 3:2-10: \xt Acts 7:30-34.\x*
\v 3 “I've got to go and¦26173 see¦26173 this¦26178 amazing sight¦26176,” Mosheh¦26169 said¦26168 \add to himself\add*. “How come that¦26178 bush¦26183 isn't¦26180 burning¦26182 up?”
\p
\v 4 When¦26185 Yahweh¦26186 saw¦26185 that¦26187 he'd left his path in order to¦26189 look¦26189, God¦26192 called¦26190 to¦26191 him¦26191 from¦26193 the¦26193 middle¦26193 of¦26193 the¦26194 bush¦26194, “Mosheh¦26196, Mosheh¦26197.”
\p “I'm¦26199 here¦26199,” he \add ≈replied¦26195\add*.
\p
\v 5 “Don't¦26202 come¦26204 any closer,” \add Yahweh\add* said¦26201. “Take¦26206 off¦26206 your¦26208 sandals¦26208 because¦26211 the¦26212 place¦26212 where you're¦26214 standing¦26215 is¦26217 HOLY ground¦26217.
\v 6 I'm¦26223 the¦26224 god of¦26224 your¦26225 father¦26225 and the¦26226 god¦26226 of¦26226 Abraham¦26227, Yitshak¦26229, and¦26230 Yacob¦26231.” \add So\add* Mosheh¦26233 covered hid¦26232 his¦26234 face¦26234 because¦26235 he¦26236 was¦26236 scared¦26236 to¦26238 look¦26237 at God¦26224.
\p
\v 7 “I've certainly noticed¦26245 the¦26248 suffering¦26262 of my people¦26249 in¦26251 Egypt¦26251,” Yahweh¦26243 \add continued\add*, “I've heard¦26255 their cries¦26254 as the \add slave drivers\add* oppress them—I'm unmistakingly aware of their pain¦26262.
\v 8 \add So\add* I've¦26264 come¦26264 down¦26264 to¦26265 set them free from¦26267 Egyptian¦26268 control and¦26269 to¦26269 bring¦26269 them up¦26269 from¦26270 \add there\add* to¦26274 a good¦26277 and¦26278 wide¦26278 land—a land flowing¦26282 with milk¦26283 and¦26284 honey¦26284. \add It's currently\add* the¦26287 place¦26287 of¦26287 the¦26288 Canaanites¦26288, the¦26289 Hittites¦26289, the¦26290 Amorites¦26290, the¦26291 Perizzites¦26291, the¦26292 Hivites¦26292, and¦26293 the¦26293 Yebusites¦26293.
\v 9 \add Yes,\add* the¦26297 cry¦26297 of¦26298 the Israelis¦26300 has¦26301 now¦26295 \add reached\add* me¦26302 and¦26303 what's more, I've¦26305 seen¦26305 \add how much\add* the¦26308 Egyptians are¦26311 oppressing¦26308 them¦26312.
\v 10 \add So\add* go¦26315 now¦26314 and¦26316 I'll¦26316 send¦26316 you¦26316 to¦26317 Far'oh¦26319 (Pharaoh) and¦26320 bring¦26320 my¦26323 people¦26324—Yisra'el's¦26326 descendants—out from¦26327 Egypt¦26327.”
\p
\v 11 “Who¦26334 am¦26335 I, that I should go¦26337 to¦26338 Far'oh¦26340,” Mosheh¦26330 \add asked\add* God¦26333, “and¦26341 that I should \add be the one to\add* bring¦26342 out¦26342 the¦26345 Israelis¦26346 out from¦26347 Egypt¦26347?”
\p
\v 12 “I'll¦26352 certainly be¦26352 with¦26361 you,” \add God replied,\add* “and¦26349 this¦26354 will¦26357 be¦26357 your sign¦26357 \add so that you'll know\add* that¦26358 I¦26359 have¦26361 sent¦26360 you¦26360: when¦26361 you¦26361 bring¦26361 the¦26364 people¦26364 out¦26361 from¦26365 Egypt¦26365, you all will¦26366 serve¦26366 God on¦26361 this \add very\add* hill¦26371.”
\rem /s1 The Divine Name Revealed
\p
\v 13 “\add But listen\add*,” Mosheh¦26375 told God¦26388, “\add If\add* I'm¦26380 about¦26381 to¦26381 go¦26381 to¦26382 the¦26384 Israelis¦26385 and¦26374 if I told them¦26387, ‘Your¦26389 ancestors¦26389'¦26389 god¦26388 has¦26390 sent¦26390 me¦26390 to¦26391 you¦26391.’ then they'd \add ask\add* me¦26394, ‘\add Oh yeah,\add* what's¦26395 his¦26397 name¦26397?’ then what would I tell¦26374 them¦26400?”\x + \xo 3:13: \xt Exo 6:2-3.\x*
\p
\v 14 “I AM WHO I AM,” God¦26403 told¦26402 Mosheh¦26406. “You'll \add tell them this\add*: ‘I AM (Yahweh) sent¦26416 me¦26416 to¦26417 you¦26412.’ ”\x + \xo 3:14: \xt Rev 1:4,8.\x*
\v 15 Then¦26419 he¦26419 \add continued\add*, “This is what you \add need\add* to tell¦26419 the¦26430 Israelis¦26431: ‘Yahweh¦26432, the¦26433 god¦26433 of¦26433 your ancestors¦26434, the¦26435 god¦26435 of¦26435 Abraham¦26436, Yitshak¦26438, and¦26439 Yacob¦26440, sent¦26441 me¦26441 to¦26442 you. This¦26443 is my name¦26445 forever, and¦26447 this¦26447 is my memorial¦26448 throughout the generations¦26450.’
\v 16 Go¦26457 and gather¦26453 the¦26456 Israeli¦26456 elders¦26456, and¦26458 tell them¦26459, ‘Yahweh¦26460, the¦26461 god¦26461 of¦26461 your ancestors¦26462, appeared¦26463 to¦26464 me¦26464, the¦26465 god¦26465 of¦26465 Abraham¦26466, Yitshak¦26467, and¦26468 Yacob¦26468, to¦26469 say¦26469, “I've¦26471 certainly noticed you all and¦26473 \add how you're all being treated\add* in¦26477 Egypt¦26477,
\v 17 and¦26479 I've¦26480 promised¦26479 to take¦26480 you¦26481 all from¦26482 your mistreatment in Egypt¦26483, away to¦26484 the¦26486 land¦26486 where the¦26487 Canaanites¦26487, Hittites¦26488, Amorites¦26489, Perizzites¦26490, Hivites¦26491, and¦26492 the¦26492 Yebusites¦26492 \add are currently living\add*—to¦26493 a land¦26495 flowing¦26496 with milk¦26497 and¦26498 honey¦26498.” ’
\p
\v 18 Then¦26500 \add the people\add* will¦26500 listen¦26500 to¦26501 what you tell them, and you and¦26504 the¦26504 Israeli¦26504 elders¦26504 will go to¦26506 the¦26508 Egyptian¦26509 king¦26508 and you'll¦26502 all tell him¦26511, ‘Yahweh, the¦26513 god¦26513 of¦26513 the¦26514 Hebrews¦26514, has¦26515 met¦26515 with¦26524 us. \add So\add* now¦26517, please¦26520 let¦26518 us¦26518 go¦26518 three¦26522 days¦26523' journey¦26521 into¦26524 the wilderness¦26524, so¦26525 we¦26525 can sacrifice¦26525 to¦26526 Yahweh, our¦26527 god.’
\v 19 \add But\add* I¦26530 know¦26530 that¦26531 the¦26536 Egyptian¦26537 king¦26536 won't¦26532 let¦26534 you¦26535 go¦26538 \add until he's forced to\add*.
\v 20 \add So I will use force\add* and I'll¦26554 strike¦26547 Egypt¦26550 with¦26551 \add several supernatural acts\add*, and¦26556 after¦26556 those, he'll¦26559 let¦26559 you¦26560 all¦26551 go¦26559.
\p
\v 21 Then¦26562 I'll¦26562 \add make\add* the¦26565 Egyptian¦26570 \add people favour you all in such a way that\add* when you go¦26573, you \add won't leave empty-handed\add*.\x + \xo 3:21-22: \xt Exo 12:35-36.\x*
\v 22 \add For example,\add* an \add Israeli\add* woman¦26579 will¦26578 ask¦26578 her¦26580 \add Egyptian\add* neighbour¦26580 (or from¦26580 someone staying \add where she lives\add*) for gold¦26587 and silver¦26585 items and for clothing¦26588. Then¦26578 you'll put¦26589 them¦26589 on¦26590 your sons¦26592 and¦26593 daughters¦26595, and¦26596 \add in this way\add* you'll all plunder¦26596 the¦26581 Egyptians¦26599.”
\c 4
\s1 God gives Mosheh three miracles
\rem /s1 Signs for Moses; Moses' Miraculous Power; Signs of the Lord's Power; God Gives Moses Miraculous Power
\p
\v 1 “But¦26601 listen,” Mosheh¦26602 responded¦26603, “they¦26607 won't believe¦26607 me¦26608 and¦26601 won't listen¦26610 to¦26611 \add what I say\add*, because¦26612 they'll¦26607 say¦26613, ‘Yahweh¦26618 never showed himself to you¦26617.’ ”
\v 2 “\add Well,\add* what's that in¦26626 your¦26626 hand¦26626?” Yahweh¦26622 \add asked\add*.
\p “\add My shepherd's\add* staff¦26628,” he¦26620 said¦26620.
\v 3 “Throw¦26631 it on the ground,” \add Yahweh\add* said¦26630. \add So Mosheh\add* he¦26633 threw¦26633 it¦26633 onto the ground and¦26635 it¦26635 became a snake¦26636, and¦26637 \add he quickly moved back away from it\add*.
\v 4 “Pick it¦26641 up by its¦26649 tail.” Yahweh¦26642 told¦26641 him¦26653. \add So Mosheh\add* picked¦26652 it up by the tail and¦26641 it¦26654 became a staff¦26655 in¦26649 his¦26656 hand¦26651 \add again\add*.
\v 5 “That's¦26658 so¦26658 that they'll believe¦26659 that¦26660 \add I\add* appeared¦26662 to¦26663 you¦26663: Yahweh¦26664—the¦26665 god¦26665 of their¦26666 ancestors¦26666—the¦26667 god¦26667 of¦26667 Abraham¦26668, Yitshak¦26670, and¦26671 Yacob¦26672.”
\p
\v 6 “\add Now\add* put¦26678 your hand \add on your chest—\add*inside your robe.” Yahweh¦26675 added. \add So Mosheh did that\add*, and¦26674 when he¦26686 pulled it¦26683 out¦26686 again¦26677, his¦26688 hand had gone \add totally\add* white with¦26682 disease.
\v 7 Then¦26692 Yahweh said¦26692, “\add Now, put\add* your¦26694 hand¦26694 \add back in again\add*.” \add So Mosheh did it again,\add* and¦26692 when he¦26698 pulled it¦26703 out¦26703, wow, it¦26707 had¦26707 \add returned to normal\add*.
\v 8 “\add So,\add* if¦26711 they don't¦26713 believe you, and¦26710 \add if the first miracle doesn't convince them\add*, then¦26710 they'll¦26714 believe \add that second miracle\add*.
\v 9 And¦26726 then, even if¦26727 they¦26730 don't¦26729 believe¦26730 those¦26734 two¦26732 miracles¦26733 and \add won't believe what you tell them\add*, then you¦26738 should take¦26738 some¦26739 water from the¦26740 river¦26740 and¦26741 pour¦26741 it¦26726 onto the dry land. Then¦26726 the water which¦26745 came from¦26747 the¦26749 river¦26749 will¦26750 \add turn into\add* blood¦26751 \add there\add* on¦26752 the dry land.”
\p
\v 10 “Oh my¦26760 master¦26760,” Mosheh¦26755 \add ≈responded¦26754\add*, “I'm not¦26761 \add very good at speaking to others—I never have been and still aren't. I seem to get tongue-tied easily\add*.”
\p
\v 11 “Who \add was it that made people's mouths\add*?” Yahweh said¦26784. “Who makes \add someone\add* mute or deaf¦26797, or¦26798 seeing¦26799 or¦26800 blind¦26801? Isn't it me, Yahweh?
\v 12 \add So\add* get going¦26807 now¦26806, and¦26808 I'll¦26809 \add look after\add* your¦26812 mouth¦26812 and¦26813 \add show\add* you¦26813 what you¦26815 to say¦26815.”
\p
\v 13 \add But Mosheh replied\add*, “Oh master¦26819, please¦26822 send¦26820 \add someone else\add*.”
\p
\v 14 Then¦26827 Yahweh¦26830 got angry¦26829 with¦26831 Mosheh¦26831 and¦26832 told¦26832 him, “Isn't Aharon¦26834 (Aaron) a fellow Levite¦26836 and your brother¦26835? I¦26837 know¦26837 that¦26838 he can speak¦26840 well¦26840, and¦26843 \add what's\add* more, he'll come¦26847 \add from Egypt\add* to¦26847 meet¦26848 you¦26848, and \add when he does, he'll be very happy\add*.
\v 15 \add He'll become your speaker\add* and¦26853 you'll¦26853 be able to talk¦26853 to¦26854 him¦26854 and \add tell him what to say\add*. I'll be¦26861 \add helping both of you speak\add* and I'll teach¦26868 you both what you should do¦26872.
\v 16 \add So in that way,\add* he'll¦26874 be¦26881 like a mouth¦26886 for you and speak¦26874 to the¦26880 people¦26880 for¦26885 you¦26885, and¦26887 you'll¦26888 be like God¦26891 to him¦26890.
\v 17 \add In addition,\add* you'll¦26897 have that¦26899 staff¦26895 with¦26898 you in¦26898 order to do¦26900 those miracles¦26905.”
\s1 Mosheh returns to Egypt
\rem /s1 Moses Returns to Egypt
\p
\v 18 Then Mosheh¦26909 went¦26908 back to¦26912 his¦26915 father-in-law¦26915 Yetro (Jethro), and¦26916 told¦26916 him¦26917, “Can I please¦26919 leave so¦26920 I can return¦26920 to¦26921 my¦26923 \add relatives\add* in Egypt¦26926 and¦26927 see¦26927 whether they're still¦26928 alive¦26929.”
\p “Go¦26933 in¦26926 peace¦26934,” Yetro \add ≈answered¦26916\add*.
\p
\v 19 \add Meanwhile\add* Yahweh¦26937 had¦26947 told¦26936 Mosheh¦26940 in¦26941 Midian¦26941, “Leave¦26942 here and¦26936 return¦26943 to Egypt¦26944 because¦26945 all¦26948 those who¦26951 \add wanted to kill you\add* are \add now\add* dead.”
\v 20 Then¦26956 Mosheh¦26957 took¦26956 his¦26960 wife¦26960 and¦26961 sons¦26963 and¦26964 put them¦26964 on¦26965 a donkey¦26967 and¦26968 returned¦26968 towards¦26969 Egypt¦26970 \add with them\add*, and¦26971 Mosheh¦26972 took¦26971 the¦26975 staff¦26975 \add that God had turned into a snake\add*.
\p
\v 21 \add On the way,\add* Yahweh¦26980 said¦26979 to¦26981 Mosheh¦26983, “When¦26979 you¦26984 return¦26985 to¦26986 Egypt¦26986, all¦26988 those miracles¦26990 that¦26991 I¦26993 gave you¦26995, you'll¦26995 be able to \add demonstrate\add* them¦26995 in¦26984 front¦26996 of¦26996 Far'oh¦26997 (Pharaoh), but¦26979 as for me, I'm going¦26984 to \add make him stubborn so that\add* he¦27004 won't¦27003 let¦27004 \add your\add* people¦27007 leave.
\v 22 \add But\add* tell¦27009 Far'oh¦27012, ‘Yahweh¦27015 has stated that Yisra'el¦27018 is¦27016 \add like\add* his oldest son¦27016
\v 23 and¦27020 he asked¦27020 you to let¦27022 that son go so¦27026 that¦27026 he¦27026 can serve¦27026 him¦27028. But¦27020 you refused¦27027 to¦27028 let¦27028 \add Yisra'el\add* go¦27028, so \add Yahweh\add* will kill¦27031 \em your¦27034\em* oldest¦27035 son.’ ”\x + \xo 4:23: \xt Exo 12:29.\x*
\p
\v 24 \add One night\add* as they were¦27037 \add overnighting at\add* a lodging¦27039 place¦27039 near the road, Yahweh¦27041 \add confronted Mosheh\add* and¦27037 would have killed¦27043 him¦27040,
\v 25 \add but his wife\add* Zipporah¦27046 took¦27045 a flint¦27047 \add knife\add* and¦27048 cut¦27048 off¦27048 her¦27052 son's¦27052 foreskin¦27051 and \add threw\add* it¦27055 at \add Mosheh's\add* feet¦27054 saying¦27055, “You're definitely a bloody¦27059 \add husband\add* to¦27061 me¦27061.”
\v 26 (That was referring to¦27069 circumcision¦27069.) \add So\add* then¦27063 \add Yahweh left them alone\add*.
\p
\v 27 \add Meanwhile\add* Yahweh¦27073 had told¦27072 Aharon¦27076, “Go¦27077 \add out\add* into the¦27080 wilderness¦27080 to¦27074 meet¦27078 Mosheh¦27079.” \add So\add* he¦27081 went¦27081 and¦27082 met¦27082 him¦27082 at¦27083 the¦27083 hill¦27083 of¦27083 God¦27084, and¦27085 he¦27085 kissed¦27085 him¦27087.
\v 28 Then¦27089 Mosheh¦27090 told¦27089 Aharon¦27091 \add everything\add* that Yahweh¦27096 had told him and how he¦27098 had¦27098 sent¦27098 him¦27098, and¦27099 all¦27100 the¦27102 miracles¦27102 that¦27103 he¦27104 commanded¦27104 him¦27104 \add to demonstrate\add*.
\p
\v 29 Then¦27106 Mosheh¦27107 and¦27108 Aharon¦27108 went¦27106 \add to Egypt\add* and¦27109 they¦27109 assembled¦27109 all¦27112 the¦27114 \add Israeli\add* elders¦27114
\v 30 and¦27131 Aharon¦27119 told¦27118 them \add everything\add* that¦27124 Yahweh¦27127 had¦27126 spoken¦27126 to¦27128 Mosheh¦27130. Then¦27118 \add Mosheh\add* did¦27131 the¦27132 miracles¦27132 in front of¦27133 \add them\add*,
\v 31 and¦27136 the¦27137 people¦27137 believed¦27136. Then¦27136 they¦27138 heard¦27138 how Yahweh¦27142 had paid attention to the¦27145 Israelis¦27146 and that¦27139 he¦27148 had¦27148 seen¦27148 their¦27151 suffering, and¦27152 they¦27152 bowed¦27152 and¦27153 prostrated themselves \add in thankfulness\add*.
\c 5
\s1 Mosheh challenges the Egyptian king
\rem /s1 Bricks Without Straw; Moses and Aaron Speak to Pharaoh; Moses and Aaron before the King of Egypt; Bricks without Straw
\p
\v 1 After¦27156 that¦27174, Mosheh¦27158 and¦27159 Aharon¦27159 went¦27157 in and¦27160 said¦27160 to¦27161 Far'oh¦27163, “This is what Yisrael's¦27169 god¦27168 Yahweh¦27167 says¦27166: ‘Let¦27170 my¦27173 people¦27173 go¦27170, \add so\add* they¦27174 can celebrate¦27174 me¦27175 \add out\add* in the¦27168 wilderness¦27176.’ ”
\p
\v 2 \add But the king\add* said¦27178, “Who's¦27180 \add this\add* ‘Yahweh’ that¦27180 I should¦27185 listen¦27183 to \add him telling me\add* to let¦27185 Yisrael¦27188 go? I don't¦27189 know¦27190 Yahweh, and¦27178 what's more, I won't¦27189 let¦27199 \add the Israelis\add* go¦27199 \add anywhere\add*.”
\p
\v 3 “The¦27202 god¦27202 of¦27202 \add us\add* Hebrews¦27203 has¦27204 met¦27204 with us,” they¦27201 \add ≈replied¦27201\add*, “Please¦27207, let¦27206 us¦27206 go¦27206 three¦27209 days¦27210 into the¦27202 wilderness¦27211 so¦27212 we¦27212 can sacrifice¦27212 to¦27213 our¦27214 god Yahweh¦27213, \add because we don't want him to strike\add* us¦27217 with¦27218 \add ≈deadly diseases\add* or¦27219 with¦27220 \add war\add*.”
\p
\v 4 “Mosheh¦27227 and¦27228 Aharon¦27228,” the king¦27224 of¦27224 Egypt¦27225 answered¦27222, “why are¦27229 you¦27229 causing¦27229 the¦27232 people¦27232 to \add slacken\add* off from¦27233 their¦27233 tasks? \add Get back\add* to¦27235 your¦27235 \add work\add*.”
\v 5 And¦27237 Far'oh¦27238 \add continued\add*, “Listen¦27239, there's many¦27241 \add of you\add* and¦27237 you \add two\add* have¦27245 caused¦27245 them¦27246 to¦27245 rest from¦27247 their¦27247 \add duties\add*.”
\rem /s1 Making Bricks without Straw
\p
\v 6 Then¦27249 on¦27256 that¦27252 \add very same\add* day¦27251, Far'oh¦27250 ordered¦27249 the¦27255 people's¦27256 slave masters and¦27257 supervisors,
\v 7 “Don't give¦27264 straw¦27265 to¦27266 the¦27268 people¦27266 to¦27267 make¦27267 bricks¦27268 with as¦27269 \add you've done up till now\add*. They¦27271 can go¦27272 and¦27273 gather¦27273 straw for¦27274 themselves.
\v 8 But¦27277 \add keep\add* the¦27279 \add same\add* quota of¦27279 bricks¦27280 \add as before\add*—don't¦27288 decrease¦27289 it¦27290 because¦27291 they \add must be\add* idle¦27293 \add if they have time to say\add*, ‘Let¦27301 us¦27301 go¦27301—let¦27302 us¦27302 sacrifice¦27302 to¦27303 our¦27303 god¦27303.’
\v 9 \add Give the men more work so they'll be too busy to worry about deceptive words\add*.”
\p
\v 10 \add So\add* the slave masters and supervisors of¦27321 the¦27322 people¦27322 went¦27320 out¦27320 and told¦27324 \add them all\add*, “\add The king\add* has said¦27324 that he¦27330 won't¦27332 give¦27333 you any more straw¦27335,
\v 11 \add so\add* you'll \add all have to\add* go¦27338 and get your own straw¦27341 from¦27342 wherever¦27342 you can find¦27343 it, because¦27344 there'll¦27345 be¦27345 no reduction to your \add quota\add*.”
\v 12 So¦27350 the¦27351 people¦27351 dispersed throughout all¦27352 of¦27354 Egypt¦27355 to¦27356 gather¦27356 stubble¦27357 for¦27358 straw¦27358,
\v 13 and¦27360 the¦27360 taskmasters¦27360 were¦27361 \add hassling\add* them¦27361, saying¦27362, “\add Hurry up and\add* finish¦27363 your¦27364 day's¦27367 work¦27364, the same as¦27369 when¦27370 \add you were given\add* straw¦27371.”
\v 14 Then¦27373 the Israeli¦27375 foremen¦27374 that Far'oh's¦27382 slave masters had¦27379 appointed¦27379 were¦27373 beaten¦27373 and¦27373 told¦27383, “Why¦27384 haven't you¦27386 met your¦27387 required¦27387 brick¦27388 making¦27388 \add quota\add* yesterday¦27393 or today? \add It's just the same as in the past\add*.”
\p
\v 15 \add So\add* the¦27399 Israeli¦27400 foremen¦27399 went¦27398 and¦27402 complained to¦27403 Far'oh¦27405, “Why¦27401 are¦27408 you¦27408 treating your¦27410 servants¦27410 like this?
\v 16 We haven't been¦27414 given¦27414 any straw¦27412 and¦27416 yet they're telling¦27417 us¦27418 to¦27415 keep making¦27419 bricks¦27416. And¦27420 see¦27420, we your servants¦27421 get beaten¦27422, but¦27416 it's your people's¦27424 mistake.”
\p
\v 17 “You're all lazy—plain lazy!” \add the king responded\add*. “That's why you're saying¦27434, ‘Let¦27435 us¦27435 go¦27435 sacrifice¦27436 to¦27437 Yahweh¦27437.’
\v 18 \add So\add* go¦27440 now¦27439! \add Back to\add* work¦27441! And¦27442 you won't¦27443 be¦27445 given¦27445 straw¦27442, but¦27439 you \add still have to meet the quota\add* of¦27447 bricks¦27448.”
\p
\v 19 \add When\add* the¦27452 Israeli¦27453 foremen¦27452 heard that the¦27453 quota wasn't going to¦27458 be reduced, they¦27451 \add realised how serious their problem was\add*,
\v 20 \add so\add* when¦27477 they¦27477 left¦27477 Far'oh¦27479 they \add confronted\add* Mosheh¦27471 and¦27468 Aharon¦27474 who were waiting to¦27476 meet¦27476 them¦27476,
\v 21 “May¦27483 Yahweh¦27484 \add see what you've done and judge you for it\add* because you've \add raised a big stink in Far'oh's mind\add* and with¦27494 his¦27495 servants¦27495. \add Now they have their excuse\add* to¦27500 kill¦27500 us¦27500!”
\s1 Mosheh complains to God
\rem /s1 Moses Complains to the Lord; God Promises Deliverance
\p
\v 22 Then¦27502 Mosheh¦27503 went back to¦27504 Yahweh¦27506 and¦27502 \add asked\add*, “My¦27508 master¦27508, why¦27509 have¦27510 you¦27510 \add taken it out on our\add* people¦27511? Was this¦27512 why you¦27515 sent¦27515 me¦27515?
\v 23 \add Ever\add* since¦27517 I¦27518 went¦27518 to¦27519 Far'oh¦27521 to¦27522 \add deliver your message\add*, he's¦27524 \add made\add* these people¦27533 \add suffer\add* and¦27517 you¦27530 \add clearly\add* haven't rescued¦27530 \add them\add*.”
\c 6
\rem /s1 Israel's Deliverance Assured; Promises of Deliverance
\p
\v 1 Then¦27535 Yahweh¦27536 said¦27535 to¦27537 Mosheh¦27539, “Now¦27540 you'll¦27541 see¦27541 what I'll¦27543 do¦27543 to¦27544 Far'oh¦27544. \add I'll use my power to force him to\add* let¦27548 them¦27548 go¦27548, and¦27535 then¦27535 \add using my power again\add* he'll¦27548 \add actually\add* drive¦27551 them¦27551 out¦27551 of¦27552 his¦27552 \add country\add*.”
\s1 God promises to rescue the people
\rem /s1 God Calls Moses
\p
\v 2 Then God¦27556 spoke¦27555 to¦27557 Mosheh¦27559 \add again\add*, “I¦27562 am¦27563 Yahweh¦27563\x + \xo 6:2-3: \xt Gen 17:1; 28:3; 35:11; Exo 3:13-15.\x*
\v 3 and¦27565 I \add revealed myself\add* to¦27566 Abraham¦27568, to¦27569 Isaac¦27571, and¦27572 to¦27572 Yacob¦27574 as El Shaddai (God¦27575 the provider) but¦27565 they didn't¦27579 know¦27580 me by my¦27577 name¦27577 Yahweh¦27578.
\v 4 \add What's more,\add* I¦27584 made¦27584 an agreement¦27587 with¦27601 them: to¦27589 give¦27589 them¦27590 the¦27593 Canaan¦27594 region¦27593—the¦27596 area which¦27598 they¦27600 had been staying¦27600 in¦27601.
\v 5 \add Furthermore,\add* I've¦27606 heard¦27606 the¦27609 complaints from the¦27610 Israelis¦27611 who¦27612 have been¦27614 enslaved by the Egyptians, and¦27616 I¦27605 have remembered¦27616 my¦27619 agreement¦27619.
\v 6 Therefore, tell¦27622 the¦27623 Israelis¦27625, ‘I am¦27628 Yahweh¦27628 and¦27629 I will¦27629 \add help you escape from\add* the¦27632 \add control\add* of¦27632 the Egyptians and¦27634 rescue¦27634 you from¦27636 their¦27636 slavery. I'll¦27629 buy you out¦27629 of¦27636 slavery using my power and¦27637 by¦27639 \add punishing the Egyptians for this injustice\add*.
\v 7 I'll take¦27644 you all to¦27646 be my own people¦27647, and¦27644 I'll be your god. Then¦27644 you'll know¦27651 that¦27652 I¦27648 am¦27654 Yahweh¦27654 your god¦27655—the one rescuing you all from¦27658 the¦27659 Egyptian¦27660 oppression.
\v 8 Then¦27662 I'll \add take\add* you all to¦27664 the¦27666 land¦27666 that¦27667 I \add promised\add* to¦27672 give¦27672 to¦27674 Abraham¦27674, to¦27675 Yitshak¦27675, and¦27676 to¦27676 Yacob¦27676, and¦27677 I'll give¦27677 it¦27678 to¦27679 you as \add your own\add*. I am¦27682 Yahweh¦27682.’ ”
\v 9 \add So\add* Mosheh¦27685 \add passed all that on\add* to¦27687 the¦27689 Israelis¦27690, but¦27684 they¦27692 wouldn't listen¦27692 to¦27693 Mosheh¦27695, because they were \add both discouraged and worn out from the slavery\add*.
\p
\v 10 Then Yahweh¦27703 told¦27707 Mosheh¦27706,
\v 11 “Go¦27709 and speak¦27710 to¦27711 the¦27714 Egyptian¦27715 king¦27714 Far'oh¦27713 and¦27716 \add tell him\add* that he¦27716 needs¦27716 to let¦27716 the¦27719 Israelis¦27721 leave¦27716 his¦27722 country¦27722.”
\v 12 And¦27724 Mosheh¦27725 said¦27728 in¦27726 front¦27726 of¦27726 Yahweh¦27727, “Look, the¦27730 Israelis¦27732 haven't listened¦27735 to¦27738 me¦27738, so why would¦27738 Far'oh¦27739 listen¦27738? And¦27740 I¦27740 have¦27735 \add difficulty speaking\add*.”
\rem /s1 Family Record of Moses and Aaron
\v 13 Then Yahweh¦27746 spoke¦27745 to¦27747 Mosheh¦27749 and¦27750 Aharon¦27752, and¦27753 he¦27753 gave them¦27753 a command¦27753 to¦27754 \add pass on to\add* the¦27756 Israelis¦27757 and¦27758 to the Egyptian¦27770 king¦27761 Far'oh¦27760: to \add take\add* the Israelis out¦27763 of¦27766 Egypt¦27762.
\s1 The ancestors of Mosheh and Aharon
\rem /s1 The Ancestors of Moses and Aaron; The Family Record of Moses and Aaron; The Genealogy of Moses and Aaron
\p
\v 14 The¦27774 following were¦27782 the leaders of¦27787 the clans¦27787:
\p The sons¦27778 of \bd Reuben\bd* (Yisra'el's¦27781 eldest¦27780 son¦27778) were Hanoch¦27782, Pallu¦27783, Hezron¦27784, and¦27785 Karmi¦27785, and \add they headed\add* the¦27787 clans of Reuben.
\p
\v 15 \bd Simeon\bd*'s sons¦27790 were¦27792 Yemuel, Yamin¦27793, Ohad¦27794, Yakin¦27795, Zohar¦27796, and¦27797 Shaul¦27797—the¦27798 son¦27798 of¦27798 a Canaanite¦27800 woman, and \add they headed\add* the¦27802 clans¦27802 of¦27802 Simeon.
\p
\v 16 \bd Levi\bd*'s sons¦27807 were¦27806 Gershon¦27811, Kohath¦27812, and¦27805 Merari¦27813. Levi was 137 years¦27814 old when he died.\x + \xo 6:16-19: \xt Num 3:17-20; 26:57-58; 1Ch 6:16-19.\x*
\v 17 Gershon's¦27823 sons¦27822 were¦27824 Libni¦27824 and¦27825 Shimei¦27825, and \add they headed\add* their¦27826 clans¦27826.
\v 18 Kohath's¦27829 sons¦27828 were¦27830 Amram¦27830, Izhar¦27831, Hebron¦27832, and¦27828 Uzziel¦27833. Kohath¦27836 was 133 years¦27834 old when he died.
\v 19 Merari's¦27843 sons¦27842 were¦27844 Mahli¦27844 and¦27845 Mushi¦27845, and \add all of those headed\add* the¦27848 clans¦27847 of¦27847 Levi¦27848.
\v 20 Amram¦27852 married his¦27856 aunt Yochebed¦27855, and¦27859 she¦27859 gave¦27859 birth¦27859 to¦27860 Aharon¦27863 and¦27864 Mosheh¦27866. Amram lived¦27868 to be¦27870 137 years¦27867 old.
\v 21 Izhar's¦27876 sons¦27875 were¦27877 Korah¦27877, Nefeg¦27878, and¦27875 Zichri¦27879.
\v 22 Uzziel's¦27882 sons¦27881 were¦27883 Mishael¦27883, Elzafan¦27884, and¦27881 Sithri¦27885.
\p
\v 23 Aharon¦27888 \add married\add* Elisheba¦27891, Amminadab's¦27894 daughter¦27892 and the¦27892 sister¦27895 of¦27892 Nahshon¦27896, and¦27899 she¦27899 gave¦27899 birth¦27899 to¦27900 Nadab¦27903, Abihu¦27906, Eleazar¦27909, and¦27910 Ithamar¦27912.
\v 24 Korah's¦27915 sons¦27914 were¦27916 Assir¦27916, Elkanah¦27917, and¦27918 Abiasaph¦27918, and \add they headed\add* the¦27920 clans¦27920 of¦27920 Korah.
\v 25 Aharon's¦27926 son¦27924 Eleazar took¦27927 a wife¦27933 for¦27933 himself from the¦27924 daughters¦27930 of¦27924 Putiel¦27931 and¦27934 she¦27934 gave¦27934 birth¦27934 to¦27935 Finehas¦27938.
\p \add All of\add* those¦27939 \add headed\add* the clans¦27943 of Levi.
\p
\v 26 It was¦27948 to¦27951 Aharon¦27946 and¦27947 Mosheh¦27947 that¦27945 Yahweh¦27950 had said¦27949, “Bring¦27952 the¦27955 Israelis¦27956 out¦27952 of¦27955 Egypt¦27958 by their¦27961 \add tribes\add*.”
\v 27 They¦27963 were¦27964 the¦27964 ones¦27964 who¦27964 spoke¦27964 to¦27965 the¦27968 Egyptian¦27977 king¦27968 Far'oh¦27967, to¦27971 bring¦27971 the¦27974 Israelis¦27976 out¦27971 of¦27974 Egypt¦27970.
\s1 God's reassurances to Mosheh and Aharon
\rem /s1 Moses and Aaron Obey God's Commands; Aaron to Speak for Moses; The Lord's Command to Moses and Aaron; The struggle with Pharaoh
\p
\v 28 On¦27983 the day¦27983 that Yahweh¦27985 spoke¦27984 to¦27986 Mosheh¦27988 in¦27989 Egypt¦27990,
\v 29 he¦27993 said¦27998, “I am¦28000 Yahweh¦27994. Tell the¦28005 Egyptian¦28006 king¦28005 Far'oh¦28004 everything that¦28010 I tell¦27998 you¦28013.”
\p
\v 30 “Please listen to me,” Mosheh¦28016 \add ≈responded¦28015\add*. “I'm¦28020 \add not a good speaker\add*, so why should Far'oh¦28026 listen to me?”
\c 7
\rem /s1 Aaron's Staff Becomes a Snake
\p
\v 1 Yahweh¦28030 \add ≈answered¦28029\add*, “Listen¦28033, I've¦28035 made you¦28035 \add like\add* God¦28036 to¦28031 Far'oh¦28037, and¦28038 your brother¦28039 Aharon¦28038 will¦28040 be¦28040 your prophet¦28041.
\v 2 You'll \add quietly\add* say everything that¦28048 I¦28049 tell you¦28049 to and¦28050 then your brother¦28051 Aharon¦28050 will¦28052 \add relay it\add* to¦28053 Far'oh¦28055 \add and tell him\add* that he¦28056 must¦28044 let¦28056 the¦28059 Israelis¦28061 leave¦28056 his¦28062 country¦28062.
\v 3 \add But\add* I'll \add make\add* Far'oh¦28069 \add stubborn\add* and \add so\add* I'll increase the¦28068 signs¦28073 and miracles¦28073 \add that I'll do\add* in¦28077 Egypt¦28078.\x + \xo 7:3: \xt Acts 7:36.\x*
\v 4 Far'oh¦28084 won't¦28080 listen¦28082 to you so I'll \add pressure\add* Egypt, and¦28080 I'll use severe judgements¦28102 \add to ensure that I\add* bring¦28090 my armies—my people¦28096 the¦28097 Israelis¦28099—out Egypt¦28101.
\v 5 The¦28121 Egyptians will¦28105 know¦28105 that¦28107 I am¦28110 Yahweh¦28110 when¦28111 I \add demonstrate my power to\add* Egypt¦28117 \add by taking\add* the Israelis¦28123 out¦28118 from¦28124 the middle of¦28121 them¦28124.”
\v 6 \add So\add* Mosheh¦28127 and¦28128 Aharon¦28128 did¦28134 everything that Yahweh¦28131 told¦28130 them¦28132 to do¦28134.
\v 7 (Mosheh¦28136 was eighty and¦28136 Aharon¦28141 was¦28142 eighty-three¦28145 when¦28147 they¦28147 were \add interacting with\add* Far'oh¦28150.)
\s1 Staffs and snakes
\rem /s1 Aaron's Staff Becomes a Snake; Aaron's Walking Stick; Aaron's Miraculous Rod
\p
\v 8 Then Yahweh¦28154 told¦28153 Mosheh¦28157 and¦28158 Aharon¦28160,
\v 9 “\add When\add* Far'oh tells¦28164 you¦28171 to¦28172 perform a miracle \add as a sign\add*, then \add just\add* tell Aharon¦28174 to take¦28175 his\f + \fr 7:9 \ft It's not clear here in this account, or even in the following events, if Mosheh and Aharon had separate miraculous sticks, or if Mosheh had (temporarily?) entrusted his original staff/rod/stick to his brother and spokesman Aharon.\f* staff¦28178 and¦28171 throw¦28179 it down in¦28180 front¦28180 of¦28180 Far'oh so it¦28183 turns into¦28184 a snake¦28184.”
\v 10 \add So\add* Mosheh¦28187 and Aharon went¦28186 in to¦28189 Far'oh's¦28191 \add courtyard\add* and¦28192 did¦28192 what Yahweh¦28197 had¦28196 told¦28196 them: Aharon threw¦28198 his staff¦28202 down¦28198 in front¦28203 of¦28203 Far'oh¦28204 and¦28205 his servants¦28206 and¦28207 it¦28207 turned into¦28208 a snake¦28208.
\v 11 Then¦28210 Far'oh¦28213 also¦28211 called¦28210 for \add his\add* wise men¦28214 and¦28215 sorcerers¦28215, and¦28216 the¦28220 Egyptian¦28221 magicians did¦28216 \add the same thing\add* using their¦28222 magic—
\v 12 each¦28226 \add of them\add* threw¦28225 down¦28225 his¦28227 staff and¦28225 they¦28228 became snakes¦28229, \add except that\add* Aharon's¦28234 staff swallowed¦28230 up¦28230 their¦28236 staffs¦28236.
\v 13 \add But\add* Far'oh's¦28240 \add mind was made up\add* and¦28238 he¦28242 \add took no notice of them\add* as¦28244 Yahweh¦28246 had¦28245 said¦28245 \add earlier\add*.
\s1 Plague 1: Water to blood
\rem /s1 A Plague of Blood; The Plague of Blood; The First Plague: Water Turned to Blood; Disasters Strike Egypt
\p
\v 14 Then Yahweh¦28250 told¦28249 Mosheh¦28253, “Far'oh¦28256 \add is very stubborn and\add* he's¦28249 refused¦28257 to¦28251 let¦28258 the¦28259 people¦28259 go¦28258.
\v 15 \add So\add* go¦28261 to¦28262 Far'oh¦28264. Listen, \add every\add* morning¦28265 he¦28267 goes¦28267 \add down\add* to¦28268 the¦28268 \add river\add*, so station yourself to¦28270 meet¦28270 him¦28270 on¦28265 the¦28273 bank¦28273. Take¦28280 the staff¦28275 with¦28265 you¦28280 that¦28276 turned¦28278 into¦28279 a snake¦28279,
\v 16 and¦28283 tell him¦28284, ‘Yahweh¦28285, the¦28286 god¦28286 of¦28286 the¦28287 Hebrews¦28287, \add previously\add* sent¦28288 me¦28288 to¦28289 you¦28289 to¦28290 say¦28290, “Let¦28292 my¦28294 people¦28294 go¦28291 \add so\add* they¦28295 can serve¦28295 me¦28295 in¦28296 the wilderness¦28296.” But¦28283 listen¦28300, until¦28301 now you haven't \add done it\add*
\v 17 so¦28305 Yahweh says¦28306, “\add Now\add* you¦28309 will¦28309 know¦28309 that¦28318 I¦28314 am¦28312 Yahweh because¦28310 of¦28320 this¦28308: See, I'm¦28311 about¦28315 to¦28315 use my¦28320 staff¦28317 to strike¦28315 the water¦28323 in¦28308 the Nile and¦28326 it will¦28326 turn¦28326 to¦28327 blood¦28327\x + \xo 7:17: \xt Rev 16:4.\x*
\v 18 and¦28329 the¦28329 fish¦28329 in¦28332 the river¦28332 will¦28333 die¦28333 and¦28334 the river will¦28334 stink¦28334. Then¦28329 \add your people\add* will¦28336 \add gag\add* to¦28338 drink¦28338 \add the\add* water¦28339 from¦28340 the¦28342 river¦28342.” ’
\p
\v 19 “Tell¦28345 Aharon¦28353 to¦28347 take¦28354 his staff¦28355 and¦28356 stretch¦28356 out¦28356 his hand¦28358 over¦28359 the¦28361 waters¦28361 of¦28361 Egypt¦28362: over¦28363 their streams, their canals¦28369, and their pools¦28372, and¦28373 over¦28373 every¦28374 reservoir of their¦28377 water¦28377, and \add the water\add* will become¦28378 blood¦28380. Then there'll be¦28381 blood¦28382 in¦28383 \add the entire country\add*—even in¦28387 wooden¦28387 or stone¦28388 containers \add of water\add*.”
\p
\v 20 \add So\add* And¦28394 Mosheh¦28393 and Aharon did¦28390 \add exactly\add* what Yahweh¦28398 had¦28397 commanded¦28397: \add Aharon\add* raised¦28399 the staff¦28400 and¦28401 struck¦28401 the¦28404 river¦28406, while Far'oh¦28408 and¦28409 his¦28410 servants¦28410 \add watched\add*, and¦28411 the \add entire\add* river turned¦28411 to¦28407 blood¦28418.
\v 21 Then¦28420 the¦28420 fish¦28420 in¦28423 the river¦28423 died¦28424 and¦28425 the river stank¦28425, so the¦28426 Egyptians couldn't drink¦28431 water¦28432 from¦28433 the¦28435 river¦28435, and¦28436 the¦28437 blood¦28437 was¦28423 all¦28438 \add over\add* Egypt¦28430.
\v 22 \add But\add* the Egyptian¦28447 sorcerers did¦28443 the same with¦28448 their¦28448 magic, and¦28449 \add so\add* Far'oh¦28452 \add stayed stubborn\add* and¦28453 wouldn't listen¦28455 to¦28456 them¦28456, just¦28457 as¦28457 Yahweh¦28459 had¦28458 said¦28458 \add earlier\add*.
\v 23 The Far'oh¦28462 turned¦28461 around and¦28463 went¦28463 \add back to\add* his house¦28466, and¦28467 he¦28469 \add put all of that out of his mind\add*.
\v 24 \add Meanwhile,\add* all¦28476 the Egyptians dug¦28475 holes around¦28479 the¦28480 river¦28480 to get water¦28481 to drink¦28482, because¦28483 river water was¦28485 undrinkable.
\s1 Plague 2: Frogs everywhere
\p
\v 25 \add So\add* a week went by since Yahweh¦28496 had struck¦28494 the¦28499 river¦28499
\c 8
\rem /s2 Frogs
\rem /s1 The Second Plague: Frogs; A Plague of Frogs
\nb
\v 1 and¦28567 \add he\add* said¦28567 to¦28569 Mosheh¦28571, “Go to Far'oh and tell¦28567 him, ‘Yahweh¦28568 says¦28572 that you \add must\add* let my people go \add so\add* they can serve me.\f + \fr 8:1 \ft This is 7:26 in some versifications (and the following three verses are 7:27, 28, & 29).\f*
\v 2 \add But\add* if you refuse to let \add them\add* go¦28608, then look, I'm about¦28606 to pound your entire territory¦28613 with frogs¦28609.
\v 3 The¦28620 frogs¦28625 will swarm in the¦28625 river, then they'll climb out and go into your house and in your bedroom and onto your bed. They'll¦28629 go into your servants' houses and among your people, and in your \add cooking\add* bowls and into your ovens.
\v 4 They'll climb on you and on your people¦28644 and¦28645 on all your servants.’ ”
\p
\v 5 Then Yahweh said¦28653 to Mosheh¦28654, “Tell¦28653 Aharon: Hold your¦28663 staff out over¦28657 the canals, the streams, and¦28662 the pools, and¦28663 cause the¦28665 frogs¦28665 to come up onto the land of¦28667 Egypt.”
\v 6 \add So\add* Aharon reached out his hand over the Egyptian water \add supplies\add* and¦28673 frogs came up and¦28675 covered the land of¦28676 Egypt.
\v 7 \add But\add* the sorcerers did the same with¦28693 their magic, and¦28689 they¦28694 \add too\add* brought up frogs¦28687 over the land of¦28689 Egypt¦28693.
\p
\v 8 Then Far'oh¦28701 called¦28702 for¦28714 Mosheh¦28698 and¦28699 Aharon¦28699, and¦28702 told \add them\add*, “Ask Yahweh to take the¦28709 frogs¦28710 away from me and from my people. Then I'll let \add your\add* people go¦28697, and they can sacrifice to Yahweh.”
\p
\v 9 Mosheh¦28720 answered, “I'll pray for you and your servants and your people, \add praying that\add* the¦28722 frogs¦28722 will keep away from¦28723 you and your homes¦28725 and¦28729 only be left in the¦28731 river. \add And I'll give you the honour of specifying when it'll happen\add*.”
\p
\v 10 “Tomorrow \add morning\add*,” \add the king replied\add*.
\p “Just as you say,” \add Mosheh responded\add*, “so that you'll know that there's no one \add else\add* like our god Yahweh.
\v 11 \add Tomorrow\add* the frogs will leave you and go away from your houses, and from your servants and your people. They'll only¦28754 be left in the river.”
\v 12 Then Mosheh and Aharon¦28768 left Far'oh and Mosheh called out¦28769 to¦28762 Yahweh¦28761 concerning the¦28776 \add retraction\add* of¦28776 the frogs that he'd sent against \add the king\add*.
\v 13 \add So\add* Yahweh did¦28786 what Mosheh had requested and the¦28798 frogs died off from the¦28799 houses and¦28800 the¦28801 courtyards, and¦28803 from the fields.
\v 14 \add The people\add* collected them into¦28821 many piles and¦28825 the¦28828 \add whole country\add* stank.
\v 15 \add But when\add* Far'oh¦28837 saw that there was¦28841 \add relief from the frogs\add*, he¦28847 \add firmed his resolve\add* and didn't¦28845 listen¦28847 to¦28848 \add Mosheh and Aharon\add*, just¦28849 as¦28849 Yahweh¦28851 had¦28850 said¦28850 \add previously\add*.
\s1 Plague 3: Lice
\rem /s1 A Plague of Gnats; The Third Plague: Gnats; The Plague of Gnats
\p
\v 16 Then Yahweh¦28856 said¦28855 to¦28857 Mosheh¦28859, “Tell¦28855 Aharon: Stretch out¦28866 your staff and strike the dust on the ground, and¦28868 it¦28869 will¦28868 turn into¦28869 lice\f + \fr 8:16 \ft The Hebrew word ‘כֵּן/כִּנִּם’ (kēn/kinnim) could refer to either lice, gnats, or fleas. However, unlike lice, gnats don't usually \+em reside\+em* on people and animals (which seems to be implied by the text).\f* all over Egypt.”
\v 17 So Aharon held out his staff and¦28889 struck the dust \add with it\add*, and¦28890 \add the dust\add* became lice on¦28890 the people¦28890 and¦28891 on¦28891 the animals. All over Egypt¦28897, the dust turned into lice
\v 18 and¦28909 the sorcerers tried to do the same with¦28910 their magic but¦28909 they were¦28918 unable to. \add So\add* there¦28923 were lice on¦28910 people¦28917 and animals
\v 19 and the¦28942 magicians said to Far'oh, “This¦28943 is God's finger.” \add But Far'oh was stubborn\add* and he didn't listen to them, just¦28943 as Yahweh had said \add previously\add*.\x + \xo 8:19: \xt Luk 11:20.\x*
\s1 Plague 4: The swarm
\rem /s1 The Fourth Plague: Flies; A Plague of Flies; The Plague of Flies
\p
\v 20 Then Yahweh said to Mosheh, “Get up early in the morning and \add when you see Far'oh\add* coming¦28949 down to¦28952 the river, station yourself in¦28962 front¦28962 of¦28962 \add him\add* and tell him, ‘Yahweh saw that you \add must\add* let my people go \add so\add* they can serve me.
\v 21 Certainly if you don't release my people immediately, then take note: I'm about¦28975 to¦28968 send the swarm\f + \fr 8:21 \ft The Hebrew word ‘עָרֹב’ (ˊārov) probably meaning ‘mixture’ is sometimes translated as ‘swarm’. This plague could have been anything from flies to other insects (hornets or mosquitoes???) all the way to a mixture of wild animals.\f* at you and your servants and at your people and into¦28975 your houses. The¦28976 Egyptians' houses will be full of¦28975 the swarm and even the ground¦28976 that they're on¦28976.
\v 22 \add But when it happens,\add* I'll distinguish the Goshen region where my people live so¦28984 the swarm won't¦28981 be¦28982 there, \add so that you'll be able to see\add* that I'm Yahweh¦28989 \add and I'm right here\add* in this land.
\v 23 This sign will¦29005 happen tomorrow and¦29007 \add you'll see how I plan to set my people free\add* from your people.’ ”
\v 24 \add So\add* Yahweh¦29021 did \add what he'd said\add* and a heavy swarm came to¦29021 Far'oh's¦29016 house and¦29020 the houses of¦29022 his servants and all the Egyptians—the country was ruined by the \add arrival\add* of the swarm.
\p
\v 25 Then Far'oh¦29046 called for¦29062 Mosheh¦29035 and to Aharon and told¦29034 \add them\add*, “Go¦29038 and sacrifice¦29061 to the¦29045 god of¦29047 you¦29039 all, \add but here in Egypt\add*.”
\p
\v 26 “It¦29065 wouldn't be right to do that,” Mosheh¦29066 \add ≈replied\add*, “because our sacrifices to¦29070 Yahweh¦29072 our god \add would be offensive\add* to Egyptians. Listen, if we made sacrifices in front of the Egyptians \add that are repulsive to them\add*, wouldn't they \add throw rocks at us to try to kill us\add*?
\v 27 We need to¦29077 travel into the¦29077 wilderness for three days, then we'll sacrifice to our god Yahweh¦29076 \add there\add*, just like he's¦29079 telling us to.”
\p
\v 28 \add So\add* Far'oh¦29090 \add relented\add*, “I'll let¦29098 you all go into the wilderness and¦29097 sacrifice to Yahweh your god—except you definitely can't¦29097 go¦29098 very far. \add Now,\add* ask God for me \add for the plague to stop\add*.”
\p
\v 29 “Listen, I'm about to \add leave now\add*,” Mosheh \add ≈responded\add*, “and I'll pray to Yahweh and he'll withdraw the swarm from Far'oh tomorrow, and from his servants and his people. \add But\add* may Far'oh not be dishonest \add again and\add* fail to let our people go to sacrifice to Yahweh.”
\p
\v 30 Then Mosheh left Far'oh and prayed to Yahweh
\v 31 and Yahweh did \add what\add* Mosheh \add asked him\add* and he withdrew the swarm from Far'oh, and from his servants and his people. Not one remained.
\v 32 \add But\add* Far'oh \add changed his mind and remained stubborn and he wouldn't let the Israelis leave\add*.
\c 9
